00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:07.749 align:start position:0%
 
hello  and thanks so much for joining for this  brief presentation  on our experiences with the epi lamp  program  and specifically how we have adapted  leadership and management training for  national immunization teams  in the time in the context of covert 19.  my name is erica lenander  i direct a group out of yale called the  global health leadership initiative  and i'm joined in this experience by my  colleagues in our partner organizations  as part of the epi lamp consortium  including representatives from the  university of global health equity in  rwanda  the university of yonde and cameroon  gavi  path and and of course our students and  alumni  in the program so the reason that um  that i want to talk with you about the  epilap experience and our adaptations in  the context of covid  is that we know that leadership and  management are essential in the covert  response  so in times of crises we really need  national immunization teams  to be able to come together solve  problems and move forward in a  coordinated national response  and yet at the same time we see  investments  in routine health system strengthening  being diverted  into more acute covent response and  our old models of training for  leadership and management just aren't  viable  in the context of covid given  restrictions on travel and convening  and so the question becomes how can we  uh  provide support for leadership and  management in this new context  so just to provide a little bit of  background on what the epilap  program is epilimp is a nine-month  certificate  program for national immunization teams  in gothi eligible countries  so we have participants come together as  teams  to build their management and leadership  capacity in the six areas  shown on the poster there strategic  problem solving leading effective teams  political engagement human resource  management financial management and  supply chain management  we've been delivering the program since  2018 we're now  offering our the program for cohorts  four and  five uh so we've reached 54 alumni and  49  current delegates across 27 gabi  eligible countries  in french and english speaking africa  and asia  obviously like most of us we were forced  to adapt the program  in the context of covet 19 and i want to  tell you a little bit about how we've  done that  and our results to date so if you zip  over to the  right hand side of the poster you see we  used two methods for adaptation  the first thing we did is we worked with  each participating country  to undergo a structured risk assessment  to understand  how were they working remotely were they  working remotely how was it going  any lessons learned there what was their  internet internet connectivity and cost  like  um would they be able to participate in  the program given competing priorities  so what would the program have to look  like to be on demand and flexible um  how might they like to collaborate we  combined that country-specific risk  assessment  with a review of the literature the  peer-reviewed literature and the gray  literature  around best practices for online  education for  professionals and as a result of those  two inputs the country-specific  assessment  and then the um kind of systematic  review of best practices  we came up with five adaptations to the  epilamp experience  that i want to talk with you about the  first is that we came up with  um with a framework we came up with a  way of approaching  um the creation of a professional  learning community  that was evidence-based so we could have  confidence and our participants could  have confidence  that we were attending to all the  different parts of  curricular approaches that would be  needed for them to be successful  we also shifted away from in-person  convenings and instead moved to  increase investment in small group  facilitation online  meaning lots of breakout rooms  investment in facilitators and those  breakout rooms that could use  miro and other knowledge capture tools  to make sure that these sessions were  really  active and engaging we updated the  curriculum with covet specific  applications so still teaching the real  essentials of management and leadership  but making sure that they were always  focused on the practical challenges  that teams were facing in the context of  the coveted response  we broadened our communication channels  to include slack  and whatsapp so that we're able to reach  delegates to reach participants  on multiple channels throughout the day  throughout the week throughout the  program  and finally we found mechanisms to get  participants cash  for mobile access so across our  participating countries  we found that actually the internet was  okay it was just expensive  and so we needed if we wanted  participants to come online  turn on their videos participate  actively we needed to make sure  that they had the financial compensation  to to buy that airtime  and turn their videos on we're just over  halfway through the program now through  cohorts four and five  with this new model and i want to share  a little bit about results today  first of all as you see in that middle  panel there we're having 88  participation in our bi-weekly live  sessions  that means that despite their busy  schedules despite  the challenges of internet connectivity  participants are coming  online and participating we think this  is because the  sessions are highly interactive because  the content is relevant and because  they're learning from  each other in those sessions we've also  seen that the demand for coaching we  provide coaching  to each one of the teams and the demand  for those coaching sessions  is twice what it has been in past  cohorts  so again even though these participants  are very busy  in the context of covid we're seeing  that they actually want more coaching  um again highlighting the relevance of  the program  and because of this new model where  things are better integrated  and we're being much more responsive  we're seeing better progress on their  breakthrough projects  um now halfway through the program  compared to prior cohorts  so this integrated virtual model of  delivery  is actually delivering better results  than the old model of bringing  participants together  for periodic in-person trainings  so what does this mean what do we take  from this experience first of all  national immunization professionals are  demanding leadership support  despite the competing challenges  associated with the cove  with cobia 19. so we thought they'd be  too busy we thought they'd be distracted  but instead they're telling us no  we need leadership support now do  not cancel this thing um the second  implication that we're seeing  is that through systematic adaptation  using the methods that i've described  earlier  virtual models can be accessible  engaging and responsive  so the virtual stuff can work as long as  you go about it  systematically and based on evidence and  lastly i think we're seeing from our  participants  a call for leadership development to  remain  part an integral part of national and  global response and recovery  thanks for taking the time to listen and  learn a little bit more about the epilip  program if you want to learn even more  scan the qr code up in the top left  corner  that will take you to a website that  will give you a little bit more  background on the program  as well as some mobile friendly content  so you don't have to squint  at this tiny little pdf poster thanks  again

